WHY THESE FILES ARE IN THE REPO RATHER THAN FETCHED AT INSTALL TIME
-------------------------------------------------------------------
This is third-party code on a mail server, so it is pinned and reviewable
rather than downloaded on every install. Upstream's own INSTALL.md instructs

    wget .../clamav-unofficial-sigs/master/clamav-unofficial-sigs.sh -O ...

which fetches the tip of master at whatever moment the install happens to run:
unpinned, unverifiable, and different on two hosts installed a day apart.
Vendoring the 8.0.0 release instead means the installer needs no network for
this step, the exact bytes are in git and diffable on upgrade, and a tampered
mirror cannot change what lands on the host. 90-verify checks SHA256SUMS.

WHY IT IS NEEDED AT ALL
-----------------------
Only on Debian/Ubuntu. EL gets the same 8.0.0 from EPEL as a real package
(clamav-unofficial-sigs-8.0.0-1.el9, verified identical apart from two EPEL
packaging defaults), and that path is unchanged. Debian and Ubuntu have no
usable package: noble and trixie onward dropped it, and the 3.7.2 still in
jammy/bookworm is a different upstream whose config layout the ilexa console
cannot drive. Without these files the console's ClamAV signature-source and
YARA panel stays hidden on every Debian/Ubuntu host.

UPGRADING
---------
Re-download the new release tarball, replace these five files, regenerate
SHA256SUMS, and update the release/commit/sha256 lines above. Diff the new
master.conf against the old one first: the console reads default_dbs_rating,
enable_yararules and the per-source *_enabled / *_dbs_rating keys from it, and
a renamed key silently changes what the panel reports.
